      <div class="section-block">
        <h2>The four big sources</h2>
        <p>These account for almost all of the extra greenhouse gas that human activity adds to the atmosphere each year. Select a tab to see how each one works.</p>
        <div class="tablist" role="tablist" aria-label="Causes of climate change">
          <button class="tab" role="tab" id="tab-energy" aria-controls="panel-energy" aria-selected="true">Energy &amp; transport</button>
          <button class="tab" role="tab" id="tab-forests" aria-controls="panel-forests" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Deforestation</button>
          <button class="tab" role="tab" id="tab-farming" aria-controls="panel-farming" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Agriculture</button>
          <button class="tab" role="tab" id="tab-industry" aria-controls="panel-industry" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Industry</button>
        </div>
        <div class="tabpanel is-active" role="tabpanel" id="panel-energy" aria-labelledby="tab-energy">
          <p class="stat-mini">The single largest source</p>
          <p>Burning coal, oil and gas for electricity, heating and transport releases carbon dioxide that had been locked away for millions of years. Petrol and diesel cars, gas boilers and coal or gas power stations are the everyday examples most people in the UK come into contact with directly.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tabpanel" role="tabpanel" id="panel-forests" aria-labelledby="tab-forests" hidden>
          <p class="stat-mini">Removes a natural carbon store</p>
          <p>Trees absorb CO2 as they grow. Clearing forests — often to create farmland — releases the carbon they were storing and removes a natural system that would otherwise keep absorbing more. Large-scale deforestation is concentrated in tropical regions such as the Amazon and parts of Southeast Asia.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tabpanel" role="tabpanel" id="panel-farming" aria-labelledby="tab-farming" hidden>
          <p class="stat-mini">A major source of methane</p>
          <p>Cattle and sheep produce methane as they digest food, and flooded rice paddies produce it too. Fertilisers used on crops release nitrous oxide. Both gases trap significantly more heat than CO2 over the short term, even though they're released in smaller quantities.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tabpanel" role="tabpanel" id="panel-industry" aria-labelledby="tab-industry" hidden>
          <p class="stat-mini">Emissions built into materials</p>
          <p>Making cement, steel and chemicals releases CO2 both from the energy used and, for cement in particular, from the chemical process itself. Because these materials are everywhere in construction, industrial emissions are harder to design out than most people expect.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
