        <div class="timeline-bars" id="tlBars" aria-hidden="true"></div>
        <p class="timeline-desc" id="tlDesc">Global average temperature was close to its long-term baseline in the early 1900s, before large-scale industrial emissions took hold.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="section-block">
        <h2>Effects around the world</h2>
        <div class="accordion" id="effectsAccordion">
          <div class="acc-item">
            <h3><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="acc-ice">Ice, oceans and sea levels<span class="plus" aria-hidden="true"></span></button></h3>
            <div class="acc-panel is-open" id="acc-ice">
              <p>Glaciers and polar ice sheets are melting faster than they're being replaced, and warmer water expands, both of which raise sea levels. Low-lying coastal towns and cities — including parts of the UK's east coast — face a growing risk of flooding.</p>
              <p>Oceans also absorb extra CO2, which makes seawater more acidic and makes it harder for coral and shellfish to build their skeletons and shells.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="acc-item">
            <h3><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="acc-weather">Weather and extreme events<span class="plus" aria-hidden="true"></span></button></h3>
            <div class="acc-panel" id="acc-weather">
              <p>Heatwaves, heavy rainfall, droughts and storms are becoming more frequent and more intense in many regions. A warmer atmosphere holds more moisture, which can mean heavier downpours and flash flooding — something the UK has seen more of in recent winters.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="acc-item">
            <h3><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="acc-nature">Ecosystems and biodiversity<span class="plus" aria-hidden="true"></span></button></h3>
            <div class="acc-panel" id="acc-nature">
              <p>Species are shifting where and when they breed, migrate and flower as temperatures change, but not all species can move or adapt fast enough. Coral reefs, Arctic habitats and many UK wildflower and insect populations are especially sensitive to these shifts.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="acc-item">
            <h3><button class="acc-trigger" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="acc-people">People, food and health<span class="plus" aria-hidden="true"></span></button></h3>
            <div class="acc-panel" id="acc-people">
              <p>Changing rainfall and rising temperatures affect crop yields and water supplies, which can drive food insecurity and migration. Heatwaves also carry direct health risks, particularly for older people and those with existing health conditions — and extreme weather brings real economic costs to households and insurers.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
